  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogrammoides_pucallpaensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Among the dwarfs this is the smallest: Apistogrammoides pucallpaensis. This species from Peru is one of the smallest species of cichlid at all. The maximum size is given in literature with 5 cm (males) and 4 cm (females). However, this must refer to very old aquarium specimens. Wild collected ones are mature and fully grown with 3 cm (males) and about 2.5 cm (females). All these sizes are total length (inclusive caudal fin).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogrammoides_pucallpaensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males and females look very much alike; males can be always recognized by the blue spots in the caudal fin, which are lacking in females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogrammoides_pucallpaensis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Wild collected animals almost always have more or less developed black spots on the body and/or fins. This is completely harmless and can be compared with freckles in humans. These black spots do not inherit and can be found in wild collected specimens only. So it is very likely that they are a skin reaction on a attack of a (unsuccessful) parasite that does not appear in aquaria. In any case animals with black spots do not show any difference to animals without black spots in respect of health and longevity.</p>"
    }
  ],
